summary_text:
On keyframe `update_parameters` likely apply=0, setup disables segmentation. If we applied ROI after, good. If we didn't call setup before ROI, CR's state not reset. For keyframe, `setup_frame` resets segmentation features, but CR internal `last_coded_q_map` not reset. It checks `if ((frame_type == KEY_FRAME...)` inside when apply=0; skipping setup could leave stale. So we should call `vp9_cyclic_refresh_setup` on keyframe even with ROI. More generally, for ROI + CYCLIC_REFRESH_AQ, we should call CR setup before applying ROI to keep CR bookkeeping. For non-intra, no skip feature, old code did not call CR setup (because ROI branch took precedence), but CR may be stale from previous frames. Existing behavior questionable. Requirement compatibility suggests always call CR setup when aq_mode CR, then apply ROI. That keeps CR state and lets ROI override segmentation. So:

```
if (cpi->oxcf.aq_mode == CYCLIC_REFRESH_AQ) {
  vp9_cyclic_refresh_setup(cpi);
}
if (cpi->roi.enabled) {
  if (skip && !intra) {
    if (frames > 20) apply;
  } else apply;
}
```
But on non-intra ROI skip before 20, calling CR setup resets segmentation to CR map, losing previous ROI QP offsets? Then no apply, so ROI QP offsets not applied before 20. Old behavior: skip path called CR setup then no ROI until 20; same. Our keyframe previously left ROI map; but with CR setup on keyframe and then apply keyframe (intra), ROI applies. Good.

For ROI + skip after 20: CR setup then apply ROI. Same.
For ROI + no skip: CR setup then apply ROI. Better bookkeeping.
For no ROI + CR: CR setup.

Thus simplify:
```
if (aq_mode == CR) vp9_cyclic_refresh_setup(cpi);
if (roi.enabled) { ... apply ...}
```
This is cleaner and more compatible. Does CR setup on keyframe disable segmentation then apply ROI; fine.

Let's patch block.

